00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
THE COST OF CHATGPT:
Have you ever wondered how much ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:11.820
engineers make? Well, the answer is $925,000. And&nbsp;
that’s not top earners or directors or executives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:20.400
That is straight up the median. The average joe&nbsp;
at OpenAI is paid $300,000 in cash and $625,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.520
stock every single year. Forget making $300 a day&nbsp;
using ChatGPT. You might as well make a million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.220
dollars making ChatGPT. Of course, you would&nbsp;
first have to become an experienced coder but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:38.700
clearly worth the investment. But, is it actually&nbsp;
worth it for OpenAI, the parent of ChatGPT? I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:43.920
just think about it. Even if OpenAI were to employ&nbsp;
just a 1000 engineers, we’re talking about annual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.780
payroll of a billion dollars. If they were to&nbsp;
grow to the scale of Google for instance, we’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:55.620
talking about nearly 200,000 employees or nearly&nbsp;
$200 billion per year in payroll. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:02.160
not all of them would be engineers, but you get&nbsp;
the idea: it’s expensive as hell. And that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.780
just half the puzzle. To develop something&nbsp;
like ChatGPT, you not only need the smartest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:12.780
human brains on the planet but you also need the&nbsp;
smartest computers on the planet. 30,000 of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:20.100
to be precise. Each of these bad boys comes in at&nbsp;
10 to 15gs so we’re talking about $300 million at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:25.680
the very least. And who knows how long they have&nbsp;
until these GPUs become outdated. Not to mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.720
all of the servers, databases, and infrastructure&nbsp;
required to support their massive operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:37.800
Thankfully they have hundreds of millions of users&nbsp;
paying the…wait a minute. It’s free. Yeah, I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:44.280
think you’d be surprised to hear that they lost&nbsp;
approximately $540 million last year. And ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.560
didn’t even come out till December, so their&nbsp;
losses this year are only gonna be exponentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:55.740
worse. Almost definitely gonna be in the billions.&nbsp;
And with how early this technology is, you can bet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:01.740
that losses will only get worse as OpenAI doubles,&nbsp;
triples, quadruples down on their R&amp;D. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:06.180
if they’re able to climb this mountain, they&nbsp;
literally have trillions waiting for them on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:11.460
the otherside. But to get there, they’re first&nbsp;
gonna have to spend hundreds of billions themself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:16.140
which brings us into the question of the day: can&nbsp;
OpenAI actually afford to see the finish line?

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.980
THEORETICAL REVENUE:
Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:25.440
to answer whether ChatGPT will actually be a&nbsp;
viable endeavor, we’ll first have to take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.020
at how ChatGPT plans on making money. They could&nbsp;
of course run ads like Google and they may very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:36.360
well do this at one point, but this doesn’t seem&nbsp;
to be their main monetization strategy, atleast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:41.460
not right now. It seems like they far prefer more&nbsp;
direct avenues of revenue like pro subscriptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.080
They currently offer a pro plan that costs&nbsp;
$20/mo. It comes with better availability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:51.420
faster responses, and early access to new&nbsp;
features. Honestly, this isn’t all that valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.340
to the casual user right now but I think its safe&nbsp;
to assume that the disparity between the free plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.440
and the pro plan will only grow with time making&nbsp;
it much more appealing. If they’re eventually able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.440
to get 200 or 300 million people to sign up for&nbsp;
this, that would pull in $50 to $70 billion per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.320
year which is phenomenal but that’s not where&nbsp;
the real money is. The real money is with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:19.440
companies. You see, OpenAI sells API access to&nbsp;
ChatGPT. This essentially means that you can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.840
requests to ChatGPT using code allowing you to&nbsp;
integrate ChatGPT into any app or website you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:31.500
building. The pricing for these requests are based&nbsp;
on tokens. 1000 tokens is approximately 750 words.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:38.040
For every 750 words worth of input, you have to&nbsp;
pay them 3 cents and for every 750 words worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:44.220
of output, you have to pay them 6 cents. That’s&nbsp;
if you want 8k worth of context. If you want 32k&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.680
worth of context, the prices double. That might&nbsp;
sound cheap and it is if you’re making hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.120
or even thousands of requests. But, when you&nbsp;
really start looking at large scale integration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:04:01.320
it becomes very expensive very quickly. Let’s take&nbsp;
a real world example: Bing. Now, Microsoft is owns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:06.960
about half of OpenAI so I’m sure they get highly&nbsp;
subsidized pricing for Bing but let’s assume they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.540
don’t just to illustrate how expensive ChatGPT&nbsp;
really is. It’s estimated that for every 100,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:19.920
requests into GPT 4 with an 8K context window,&nbsp;
the API costs $7,500. Bing gets over 900&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:27.360
million searches every single day which translates&nbsp;
to $67.5 million. Over a year, we’re talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.200
just under $25 billion and that’s just from Bing.&nbsp;
Imagine if Google integrated GPT 4. Every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:40.440
they get 8.5 billion searches, so they’d have&nbsp;
to pay OpenAI over $600 million every day or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:46.980
$230 billion every year. Of course, that would&nbsp;
be unsustainable for Google and there’s no way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:51.660
they’ll be paying those prices. But I think you&nbsp;
can see how big companies could easily end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.860
paying billions if not tens of billions to OpenAI&nbsp;
every single year for large scale integration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:01.740
If you get a few hundred of these big boys on&nbsp;
your side like Oracle or AWS or SAP have, you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.660
be pulling in hundreds of billions per year and&nbsp;
eventually trillions. So, theoretically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:13.500
not only does OpenAI has a viable business model&nbsp;
but a super lucrative one. There’s just one teensy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:19.380
tiny problem though, getting these companies on&nbsp;
board is not only hard but extremely expensive.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:25.020
VERY REAL COSTS:
For one, many of the biggest companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:30.240
in the world will try to directly compete with&nbsp;
ChatGPT. Google is already doing it with Bard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.560
even Meta is trying to create their own language&nbsp;
model. So, you can bet that these companies wont&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:39.120
be adopting OpenAI’s solutions anytime soon.&nbsp;
Fortunately, there are still plenty of large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.200
companies out there that aren’t creating their&nbsp;
own language model but could very much benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:49.080
from one. Likely the best example is Apple. In&nbsp;
the most recent WWDC, they didn’t even mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:54.900
the term AI much less an AI language model. Now,&nbsp;
they could very well be working on a model behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:00.120
the scenes for Siri but I think they’re taking&nbsp;
a different approach. Just a few weeks ago, Tim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.820
Cook said that he likes ChatGPT and that he uses&nbsp;
it personally. But, just a few weeks before that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:11.280
Apple actually banned ChatGPT internally. You’re&nbsp;
probably wondering why Apple is sending these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.220
mixed signals. Well, they’re basically saying: we&nbsp;
like ChatGPT and may even be willing to integrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:23.940
it but only for the right price. And when I say&nbsp;
the right price, I don’t mean cheap or even free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:31.560
calls to ChatGPT. I mean, ChatGPT pays Apple for&nbsp;
them to use it. This probably makes no sense. Why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:38.280
in the world would ChatGPT pay for Apple to use&nbsp;
them? Well, the same reason that Google does. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:43.020
know how Siri and Safari automatically redirect&nbsp;
you to Google search? Well, this isn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:47.700
because Google is Tim’s preferred search engine or&nbsp;
something. It’s because Google hands over Apple a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:54.240
casual $20 billion per year. And that’s just to&nbsp;
stay out of the search space. Imagine how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:59.400
Apple would want to stay out of the language model&nbsp;
space. Safe to say, more than $20 billion per year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.740
over the long term. Now OpenAI could just avoid&nbsp;
doing business with companies like Apple. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:09.840
you can bet that if OpenAI doesn’t make a deal&nbsp;
with Apple, Google will. They already have a deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.760
with Apple and paying them an extra $20 or $30&nbsp;
billion every year to integrate Bard into Siri and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:20.520
Safari is a no brainer. That’s literally makes&nbsp;
Bard the default AI for over a billion people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.220
basically overnight. If you monetize these users&nbsp;
well, that’ll produce way more than the $20 or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:31.320
$30 billion they pay Apple every year. But getting&nbsp;
to that point takes time during which you’ll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:36.720
to continue paying Apple. Again, for Google, the&nbsp;
trillion dollar giant, that’s no problem. But, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:42.300
OpenAI, we’re talking about their entire valuation&nbsp;
being handed over to Apple every single year. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.320
that’s just half the equation. You can bet&nbsp;
that someone like Apple wont be willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.180
deal with error messages like these. If they’re&nbsp;
gonna push your product to their billion users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:56.100
they’re gonna first want assurance that you can&nbsp;
handle that sort of load. Fortunately, we already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.100 --> 00:08:02.340
know how to handle these loads… it’s just really&nbsp;
really expensive. Google regularly spends $30&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:07.500
billion just upgrading infrastructure. OpenAI&nbsp;
would have to build it from scratch or rent it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:13.140
from someone like Google or Amazon which will only&nbsp;
be more expensive. So, not only will OpenAI have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:17.460
to spend tens of billions on customer acquisition&nbsp;
but they’ll also have to spend tens of billions on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.460 --> 00:08:22.740
supporting these customers and their insane scale.&nbsp;
At that rate, Microsoft’s massive $10 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.180
investment wouldn’t even last a couple months.&nbsp;
I think you can start to see the problem.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:30.780
ABSOLUTELY UNVIABLE:&nbsp;

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:36.840
Everything we’ve discussed so far is simply the&nbsp;
cost to acquire and maintain customers. But with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.900
product like ChatGPT, R&amp;D is even more important.&nbsp;
All of these deals and partnerships only work if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:48.600
you continuously have a market leading product.&nbsp;
And let’s just say, the competition is by no means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.420
holding back. I mean just think about this. Our&nbsp;
boy Mark is willing to spend $100 billion on VR.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:59.040
How much do you think he’s willing to spend&nbsp;
on AI? Oh wait, we have an official number.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:06.540
It's $33 billion this year alone. Again, that’s&nbsp;
more than the entire market cap of OpenAI. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:10.800
just because you spend more money doesn’t&nbsp;
mean you’ll come out ahead but then again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:16.800
it often does. The bottom line is that the AI game&nbsp;
is simply being played on a different magnitude.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:21.840
Traditional startups need hundreds of millions&nbsp;
of dollars. OpenAI needs hundreds of billions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:28.140
dollars and that’s not my opinion. Sam Altman, the&nbsp;
CEO of OpenAI himself has suggested that they’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:32.820
need a $100 billion in the coming years itself.&nbsp;
So, in the next decade or so, we’re looking at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:38.640
$300 or $500 billion or some crazy number like&nbsp;
that. And the obvious reality is that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:43.560
absolutely no way that they’ll be able to afford&nbsp;
these numbers by themselves anytime soon. I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:49.140
the company literally pulled in a whopping $28&nbsp;
million last year. This is expected to grow to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:53.820
$40 billion by the end of this decade but in the&nbsp;
context of a multi hundred billion investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:59.760
that’s still not all that great. In fact, it may&nbsp;
not even be enough to achieve profitability. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:05.040
OpenAI is by no means a sustainable business&nbsp;
by any stretch of the imagination. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:09.660
way that OpenAI works is that you get a bunch&nbsp;
of people to invest hundreds of billions maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:15.180
even nearly a trillion dollars in the hopes of&nbsp;
making tens of trillions. Even the richest VCs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:18.840
and hedge funds can’t afford this. There’s&nbsp;
only two groups of people who can afford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:23.280
this. The first is governments but they’re&nbsp;
still stuck on Internet Explorer so they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:28.500
out of the question. The other is big tech&nbsp;
companies. OpenAI realized this 4 years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:33.780
That’s why they stopped being a non profit and&nbsp;
partnered with Microsoft. And let me just say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:37.860
